1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,964
Some of the tallest dams in the

2
00:00:01,964 --> 00:00:04,562
world are thinner than you would believe.

3
00:00:04,742 --> 00:00:07,100
A wall of concrete can hold back

4
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:09,457
a lake billions of tonnes heavy,

5
00:00:09,457 --> 00:00:12,036
while being only a few meters thick

6
00:00:12,036 --> 00:00:12,846
at the top.

7
00:00:13,026 --> 00:00:14,918
The secret is the curve.

8
00:00:15,098 --> 00:00:16,698
Bend a dam the right way,

9
00:00:16,698 --> 00:00:19,192
and the water pushes it tighter against

10
00:00:19,192 --> 00:00:21,879
the mountains instead of knocking it over.

11
00:00:22,059 --> 00:00:24,911
Today, eleven parts of a dam,

12
00:00:24,911 --> 00:00:27,272
and why each one exists.

13
00:00:27,621 --> 00:00:31,246
Dams hold back rivers for drinking water,

14
00:00:31,246 --> 00:00:34,518
farms, flood control and electricity.

15
00:00:34,698 --> 00:00:37,264
Behind each one sits a huge weight

16
00:00:37,264 --> 00:00:40,207
of water, pressing harder the deeper it

17
00:00:40,207 --> 00:00:40,584
gets.

18
00:00:40,764 --> 00:00:42,386
If a big dam fails,

19
00:00:42,386 --> 00:00:45,287
the flood downstream can be one of

20
00:00:45,287 --> 00:00:48,787
the deadliest disasters humans can cause.

21
00:00:48,967 --> 00:00:51,468
So every part of a dam exists

22
00:00:51,468 --> 00:00:52,589
for a reason.

23
00:00:52,939 --> 00:00:55,960
As always, every fact comes from a

24
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:58,449
named source, dam operators,

25
00:00:58,449 --> 00:01:03,426
government agencies, and official investigation reports,

26
00:01:03,426 --> 00:01:06,093
all listed in the description.

27
00:01:06,273 --> 00:01:07,898
Let's start with the curve.

28
00:01:08,247 --> 00:01:10,284
Why are so many dams curved,

29
00:01:10,284 --> 00:01:13,193
with the bulge pointing toward the lake?

30
00:01:13,373 --> 00:01:15,832
Because the curve turns the push of

31
00:01:15,832 --> 00:01:17,588
the water into squeezing.

32
00:01:17,768 --> 00:01:19,977
A straight wall would be bent by

33
00:01:19,977 --> 00:01:21,564
the water like a ruler.

34
00:01:21,744 --> 00:01:23,904
An arch, curved upstream,

35
00:01:23,904 --> 00:01:27,445
sends that force sideways along the curve

36
00:01:27,445 --> 00:01:30,382
into the rock walls of the canyon.

37
00:01:30,562 --> 00:01:33,721
Concrete is very strong when squeezed,

38
00:01:33,721 --> 00:01:36,797
so the arch can be surprisingly thin.

39
00:01:37,146 --> 00:01:39,211
It is the same idea as a

40
00:01:39,211 --> 00:01:41,792
stone arch in an old bridge or

41
00:01:41,792 --> 00:01:42,394
church.

42
00:01:42,574 --> 00:01:44,664
Push on the outside of an arch,

43
00:01:44,664 --> 00:01:47,225
and every block is pressed against its

44
00:01:47,225 --> 00:01:50,190
neighbors, holding the whole shape together.

45
00:01:50,370 --> 00:01:52,568
An arch dam is just an arch

46
00:01:52,568 --> 00:01:54,034
lying on its side,

47
00:01:54,034 --> 00:01:57,127
with the canyon walls as its supports.

48
00:01:57,478 --> 00:02:00,034
That is why arch dams are built

49
00:02:00,034 --> 00:02:02,921
in narrow canyons with strong rock,

50
00:02:02,921 --> 00:02:05,807
like the Contra Dam in Switzerland,

51
00:02:05,807 --> 00:02:08,694
two hundred and twenty meters tall,

52
00:02:08,694 --> 00:02:11,910
where James Bond made his famous bungee

53
00:02:11,910 --> 00:02:13,395
jump in GoldenEye.

54
00:02:13,575 --> 00:02:15,841
In a wide valley with weak rock,

55
00:02:15,841 --> 00:02:18,390
the arch would have nothing solid to

56
00:02:18,390 --> 00:02:19,310
push against.

57
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:23,018
Why are some dams straight and enormously

58
00:02:23,018 --> 00:02:25,721
thick instead of curved and thin?

59
00:02:25,901 --> 00:02:28,146
Because they use a different trick,

60
00:02:28,146 --> 00:02:28,594
weight.

61
00:02:28,774 --> 00:02:31,293
A gravity dam simply stands in the

62
00:02:31,293 --> 00:02:33,219
way of the water and is so

63
00:02:33,219 --> 00:02:35,811
heavy that the water cannot push it

64
00:02:35,811 --> 00:02:38,329
over or slide it along the ground.

65
00:02:38,509 --> 00:02:40,558
It is shaped like a triangle,

66
00:02:40,558 --> 00:02:42,606
thin at the top and very wide

67
00:02:42,606 --> 00:02:43,454
at the base,

68
00:02:43,454 --> 00:02:46,068
where the water pressure is greatest.

69
00:02:46,418 --> 00:02:49,476
Gravity dams work in wide valleys where

70
00:02:49,476 --> 00:02:51,201
an arch would not fit.

71
00:02:51,381 --> 00:02:54,016
China's Three Gorges Dam is one,

72
00:02:54,016 --> 00:02:57,228
more than two kilometers long and about

73
00:02:57,228 --> 00:03:00,110
one hundred and eighty meters tall,

74
00:03:00,110 --> 00:03:03,075
built with tens of millions of cubic

75
00:03:03,075 --> 00:03:04,639
meters of concrete.

76
00:03:04,819 --> 00:03:07,680
It is also the largest power station

77
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:08,395
on Earth.

78
00:03:08,745 --> 00:03:11,822
Many dams combine both ideas.

79
00:03:12,002 --> 00:03:14,876
Hoover Dam, on the Colorado River,

80
00:03:14,876 --> 00:03:16,820
is an arch gravity dam.

81
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:17,953
It is curved,

82
00:03:17,953 --> 00:03:20,370
so part of the water's force goes

83
00:03:20,370 --> 00:03:21,982
into the canyon walls,

84
00:03:21,982 --> 00:03:23,520
and it is very thick,

85
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:26,230
about two hundred meters at the base,

86
00:03:26,230 --> 00:03:28,355
so its weight holds the rest.

87
00:03:28,704 --> 00:03:31,330
Why is Hoover Dam full of pipes

88
00:03:31,330 --> 00:03:33,279
buried in its concrete?

89
00:03:33,459 --> 00:03:36,605
Because concrete gets hot as it hardens.

90
00:03:36,785 --> 00:03:40,541
The chemical reaction between cement and water

91
00:03:40,541 --> 00:03:41,685
releases heat.

92
00:03:41,865 --> 00:03:43,068
In a small slab,

93
00:03:43,068 --> 00:03:45,022
that heat escapes quickly.

94
00:03:45,202 --> 00:03:48,192
But engineers calculated that if Hoover Dam

95
00:03:48,192 --> 00:03:50,626
had been poured as one solid block,

96
00:03:50,626 --> 00:03:52,781
it would have taken more than a

97
00:03:52,781 --> 00:03:54,658
hundred years to cool down,

98
00:03:54,658 --> 00:03:56,813
and it would have cracked as it

99
00:03:56,813 --> 00:03:57,300
shrank.

100
00:03:57,649 --> 00:03:59,836
So the dam was built as many

101
00:03:59,836 --> 00:04:02,569
separate columns, poured in blocks,

102
00:04:02,569 --> 00:04:05,771
with about a thousand kilometers of steel

103
00:04:05,771 --> 00:04:06,787
pipes inside.

104
00:04:06,967 --> 00:04:09,747
River water and then chilled water from

105
00:04:09,747 --> 00:04:12,885
a refrigeration plant was pumped through the

106
00:04:12,885 --> 00:04:16,094
pipes, cooling the concrete in months instead

107
00:04:16,094 --> 00:04:17,021
of a century.

108
00:04:17,201 --> 00:04:20,266
Afterwards, the pipes were filled with grout.

109
00:04:20,615 --> 00:04:23,474
The same problem exists in every big

110
00:04:23,474 --> 00:04:25,062
concrete pour today.

111
00:04:25,242 --> 00:04:29,646
Large dams, bridge foundations and building cores

112
00:04:29,646 --> 00:04:32,162
are still cooled with pipes,

113
00:04:32,162 --> 00:04:33,510
ice in the mix,

114
00:04:33,510 --> 00:04:35,308
or poured in layers.

115
00:04:35,488 --> 00:04:38,187
Hidden inside, a dam is as much

116
00:04:38,187 --> 00:04:40,538
a cooling system as a wall.

117
00:04:40,888 --> 00:04:42,918
Why does a dam have a huge

118
00:04:42,918 --> 00:04:45,338
concrete channel down its side,

119
00:04:45,338 --> 00:04:46,821
like a water slide?

120
00:04:47,001 --> 00:04:49,529
Because the most dangerous thing for a

121
00:04:49,529 --> 00:04:51,592
dam is water going over the top

122
00:04:51,592 --> 00:04:52,922
where it should not.

123
00:04:53,102 --> 00:04:54,236
In a big flood,

124
00:04:54,236 --> 00:04:57,182
the lake fills faster than the turbines

125
00:04:57,182 --> 00:04:58,013
can use it.

126
00:04:58,193 --> 00:05:01,237
The spillway lets the extra water out

127
00:05:01,237 --> 00:05:03,787
safely, around or over the dam,

128
00:05:03,787 --> 00:05:06,831
without washing away its foundations.

129
00:05:07,181 --> 00:05:08,766
For an earth dam,

130
00:05:08,766 --> 00:05:10,816
made of soil and rock,

131
00:05:10,816 --> 00:05:14,171
water flowing over the top is fatal.

132
00:05:14,351 --> 00:05:16,651
It erodes the dam from the top

133
00:05:16,651 --> 00:05:18,031
down within hours.

134
00:05:18,211 --> 00:05:21,379
So spillways are designed for floods far

135
00:05:21,379 --> 00:05:23,359
bigger than any recorded,

136
00:05:23,359 --> 00:05:27,081
sometimes the largest flood thought possible at

137
00:05:27,081 --> 00:05:27,952
that river.

138
00:05:28,301 --> 00:05:31,111
In February two thousand seventeen,

139
00:05:31,111 --> 00:05:34,883
the main spillway of California's Oroville Dam,

140
00:05:34,883 --> 00:05:37,853
the tallest dam in the United States,

141
00:05:37,853 --> 00:05:40,341
broke apart during heavy rains.

142
00:05:40,521 --> 00:05:43,856
Water then began flowing down the emergency

143
00:05:43,856 --> 00:05:46,338
spillway, which started eroding.

144
00:05:46,518 --> 00:05:50,117
Nearly two hundred thousand people were evacuated.

145
00:05:50,297 --> 00:05:51,278
The dam held,

146
00:05:51,278 --> 00:05:53,919
but the spillway had to be rebuilt.

147
00:05:54,268 --> 00:05:57,098
Why do some spillway tunnels have slots

148
00:05:57,098 --> 00:05:59,203
that suck air into the water?

149
00:05:59,383 --> 00:06:02,425
Because fast water can eat concrete.

150
00:06:02,605 --> 00:06:05,295
When water flows very fast over a

151
00:06:05,295 --> 00:06:08,312
small bump, the pressure drops so low

152
00:06:08,312 --> 00:06:10,921
that tiny bubbles of vapor form,

153
00:06:10,921 --> 00:06:13,204
and then collapse violently.

154
00:06:13,384 --> 00:06:15,265
This is called cavitation.

155
00:06:15,445 --> 00:06:18,390
Each collapse is a tiny hammer blow,

156
00:06:18,390 --> 00:06:21,252
and millions of them can tear holes

157
00:06:21,252 --> 00:06:22,725
in solid concrete.

158
00:06:23,074 --> 00:06:25,255
In nineteen eighty three,

159
00:06:25,255 --> 00:06:28,744
a huge snowmelt flood forced Glen Canyon

160
00:06:28,744 --> 00:06:30,139
Dam, in Arizona,

161
00:06:30,139 --> 00:06:33,454
to use its spillway tunnels for weeks.

162
00:06:33,634 --> 00:06:36,185
Cavitation tore out concrete,

163
00:06:36,185 --> 00:06:37,945
steel and even rock,

164
00:06:37,945 --> 00:06:40,936
and the tunnels shook and spat out

165
00:06:40,936 --> 00:06:41,552
debris.

166
00:06:41,732 --> 00:06:44,652
Workers even added plywood boards on top

167
00:06:44,652 --> 00:06:47,133
of the spillway gates to hold back

168
00:06:47,133 --> 00:06:48,593
a little more water.

169
00:06:48,943 --> 00:06:50,370
After the flood,

170
00:06:50,370 --> 00:06:53,135
engineers added aeration slots.

171
00:06:53,315 --> 00:06:55,540
Air mixed into the water acts as

172
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:58,530
a cushion, so the collapsing bubbles cannot

173
00:06:58,530 --> 00:07:00,269
hit the concrete so hard.

174
00:07:00,449 --> 00:07:02,191
It is an elegant fix.

175
00:07:02,371 --> 00:07:04,757
To protect the tunnel from water,

176
00:07:04,757 --> 00:07:05,842
you let air in.

177
00:07:06,191 --> 00:07:08,476
Why are there tall towers standing in

178
00:07:08,476 --> 00:07:10,452
the lake in front of Hoover Dam?

179
00:07:10,632 --> 00:07:12,676
They are intake towers.

180
00:07:12,856 --> 00:07:15,288
They let water into the dam's power

181
00:07:15,288 --> 00:07:18,208
plant, through gates at different heights.

182
00:07:18,388 --> 00:07:21,615
Taking water from the right depth matters.

183
00:07:21,795 --> 00:07:24,326
Water near the bottom may be colder

184
00:07:24,326 --> 00:07:25,772
and carry more silt,

185
00:07:25,772 --> 00:07:28,302
while water near the surface may be

186
00:07:28,302 --> 00:07:28,808
warmer.

187
00:07:29,157 --> 00:07:32,008
Hoover Dam has four of these towers,

188
00:07:32,008 --> 00:07:34,700
each one taller than a twenty five

189
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:38,263
story building, with huge cylinder gates that

190
00:07:38,263 --> 00:07:40,164
can be opened or closed.

191
00:07:40,344 --> 00:07:42,965
Water flows from them through tunnels in

192
00:07:42,965 --> 00:07:45,522
the canyon walls to the turbines below.

193
00:07:45,702 --> 00:07:48,495
Screens on the towers stop logs and

194
00:07:48,495 --> 00:07:50,172
debris from going in.

195
00:07:50,521 --> 00:07:53,735
Many dams have intake structures like these,

196
00:07:53,735 --> 00:07:55,560
but few are as beautiful.

197
00:07:55,740 --> 00:07:58,489
Hoover Dam was built in the nineteen

198
00:07:58,489 --> 00:08:00,855
thirties with art deco details,

199
00:08:00,855 --> 00:08:03,375
and even its clocks on the towers

200
00:08:03,375 --> 00:08:05,284
show two different times.

201
00:08:05,464 --> 00:08:08,238
One for Nevada and one for Arizona,

202
00:08:08,238 --> 00:08:10,774
the two states on either side of

203
00:08:10,774 --> 00:08:11,408
the dam.

204
00:08:11,757 --> 00:08:14,419
Why are the pipes that carry water

205
00:08:14,419 --> 00:08:16,610
to a dam's turbines so huge?

206
00:08:16,790 --> 00:08:18,605
They are called penstocks,

207
00:08:18,605 --> 00:08:21,326
and they have to carry enormous amounts

208
00:08:21,326 --> 00:08:23,350
of water under high pressure.

209
00:08:23,530 --> 00:08:24,614
At Hoover Dam,

210
00:08:24,614 --> 00:08:27,712
the main penstocks are about nine meters

211
00:08:27,712 --> 00:08:30,422
across, big enough to drive a truck

212
00:08:30,422 --> 00:08:31,042
through.

213
00:08:31,222 --> 00:08:32,659
The bigger the pipe,

214
00:08:32,659 --> 00:08:35,102
the slower the water flows for the

215
00:08:35,102 --> 00:08:37,616
same amount, and the less energy is

216
00:08:37,616 --> 00:08:38,838
lost to friction.

217
00:08:39,187 --> 00:08:41,320
The weight of the lake behind the

218
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:43,518
dam is what makes the electricity.

219
00:08:43,698 --> 00:08:46,206
The deeper the turbines sit below the

220
00:08:46,206 --> 00:08:49,121
surface, the more pressure pushes the water

221
00:08:49,121 --> 00:08:50,002
through them.

222
00:08:50,182 --> 00:08:51,261
At the bottom,

223
00:08:51,261 --> 00:08:53,494
the water spins the turbines,

224
00:08:53,494 --> 00:08:55,189
which turn generators,

225
00:08:55,189 --> 00:08:57,731
and then flows out into the river

226
00:08:57,731 --> 00:08:58,193
below.

227
00:08:58,543 --> 00:09:01,446
Closing a penstock gate suddenly would be

228
00:09:01,446 --> 00:09:02,154
dangerous.

229
00:09:02,334 --> 00:09:05,019
All that moving water would slam to

230
00:09:05,019 --> 00:09:08,088
a stop, creating a pressure shock called

231
00:09:08,088 --> 00:09:11,157
water hammer, strong enough to burst the

232
00:09:11,157 --> 00:09:11,541
pipe.

233
00:09:11,721 --> 00:09:13,579
So gates close slowly,

234
00:09:13,579 --> 00:09:16,703
and many plants have surge tanks that

235
00:09:16,703 --> 00:09:19,237
let the water rise and fall to

236
00:09:19,237 --> 00:09:20,672
absorb the shock.

237
00:09:21,022 --> 00:09:23,532
Why are there tunnels running through the

238
00:09:23,532 --> 00:09:25,063
inside of a concrete dam?

239
00:09:25,243 --> 00:09:27,414
They are inspection galleries.

240
00:09:27,594 --> 00:09:30,450
Engineers walk through them to check the

241
00:09:30,450 --> 00:09:31,878
dam from the inside.

242
00:09:32,058 --> 00:09:33,543
They measure cracks,

243
00:09:33,543 --> 00:09:37,552
temperature, movement, and especially water that seeps

244
00:09:37,552 --> 00:09:40,522
through the concrete and the rock below.

245
00:09:40,702 --> 00:09:42,779
A dam is not supposed to be

246
00:09:42,779 --> 00:09:46,472
perfectly watertight, but leaks must be watched.

247
00:09:46,821 --> 00:09:49,425
Water seeping under a dam can push

248
00:09:49,425 --> 00:09:50,420
upward on it,

249
00:09:50,420 --> 00:09:52,870
like the dam is trying to float.

250
00:09:53,050 --> 00:09:54,587
This is called uplift,

251
00:09:54,587 --> 00:09:56,822
and it reduces the dam's grip on

252
00:09:56,822 --> 00:09:57,590
the ground.

253
00:09:57,770 --> 00:10:00,498
So drains are drilled from the galleries

254
00:10:00,498 --> 00:10:01,861
into the rock below,

255
00:10:01,861 --> 00:10:04,726
letting that water escape and lowering the

256
00:10:04,726 --> 00:10:05,339
pressure.

257
00:10:05,689 --> 00:10:07,079
Inside many dams,

258
00:10:07,079 --> 00:10:10,431
there are also hanging wires called plumb

259
00:10:10,431 --> 00:10:13,210
lines, which show if the dam leans

260
00:10:13,210 --> 00:10:16,072
a few millimeters more in summer or

261
00:10:16,072 --> 00:10:17,543
under a full lake.

262
00:10:17,723 --> 00:10:19,928
Big dams bend a little with the

263
00:10:19,928 --> 00:10:22,204
water level and the temperature,

264
00:10:22,204 --> 00:10:25,404
and engineers have records of those movements

265
00:10:25,404 --> 00:10:26,756
going back decades.

266
00:10:27,105 --> 00:10:29,858
Why do some dams have a staircase

267
00:10:29,858 --> 00:10:32,109
of small pools beside them?

268
00:10:32,289 --> 00:10:33,775
It is a fish ladder.

269
00:10:33,955 --> 00:10:36,525
Salmon and other fish swim upstream to

270
00:10:36,525 --> 00:10:38,959
lay their eggs where they were born.

271
00:10:39,139 --> 00:10:41,682
A dam blocks their path completely.

272
00:10:41,862 --> 00:10:44,696
A fish ladder gives them a route

273
00:10:44,696 --> 00:10:46,024
of small steps,

274
00:10:46,024 --> 00:10:47,441
each one a pool,

275
00:10:47,441 --> 00:10:50,098
that they can jump or swim up,

276
00:10:50,098 --> 00:10:51,870
one level at a time.

277
00:10:52,219 --> 00:10:53,941
On the Columbia River,

278
00:10:53,941 --> 00:10:55,585
in the United States,

279
00:10:55,585 --> 00:10:58,873
big dams like Bonneville have fish ladders

280
00:10:58,873 --> 00:11:02,317
with windows where visitors can watch salmon

281
00:11:02,317 --> 00:11:04,352
pass and staff count them.

282
00:11:04,532 --> 00:11:07,728
Some dams even use fish elevators,

283
00:11:07,728 --> 00:11:10,830
or trucks, to carry fish over the

284
00:11:10,830 --> 00:11:11,300
wall.

285
00:11:11,650 --> 00:11:15,093
Getting young fish back downstream is just

286
00:11:15,093 --> 00:11:18,618
as hard, because the turbines and spillways

287
00:11:18,618 --> 00:11:19,766
can hurt them.

288
00:11:19,946 --> 00:11:22,326
So modern dams use screens,

289
00:11:22,326 --> 00:11:26,204
bypass channels, and fish friendly turbines.

290
00:11:26,384 --> 00:11:28,719
A dam is not just a wall

291
00:11:28,719 --> 00:11:29,692
for water.

292
00:11:29,872 --> 00:11:32,036
It is a wall in the middle

293
00:11:32,036 --> 00:11:33,367
of an ecosystem.

294
00:11:33,717 --> 00:11:35,900
Why is the Vajont Dam in Italy

295
00:11:35,900 --> 00:11:39,318
still standing, even though nearly two thousand

296
00:11:39,318 --> 00:11:40,846
people died below it?

297
00:11:41,026 --> 00:11:42,930
Because the dam did not fail.

298
00:11:43,110 --> 00:11:45,592
In October nineteen sixty three,

299
00:11:45,592 --> 00:11:46,910
a huge landslide,

300
00:11:46,910 --> 00:11:49,935
more than two hundred and fifty million

301
00:11:49,935 --> 00:11:51,563
cubic meters of rock,

302
00:11:51,563 --> 00:11:54,200
slid from a mountain into the lake

303
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:54,975
behind it.

304
00:11:55,155 --> 00:11:57,345
It pushed a wave of water over

305
00:11:57,345 --> 00:11:58,731
the top of the dam.

306
00:11:59,081 --> 00:12:01,481
The wave rose far above the dam

307
00:12:01,481 --> 00:12:04,422
and fell into the narrow valley below,

308
00:12:04,422 --> 00:12:07,750
destroying the town of Longarone and nearby

309
00:12:07,750 --> 00:12:09,298
villages in minutes.

310
00:12:09,478 --> 00:12:11,471
The dam, a thin arch,

311
00:12:11,471 --> 00:12:13,275
was barely damaged.

312
00:12:13,455 --> 00:12:16,144
Warning signs of the moving mountain had

313
00:12:16,144 --> 00:12:17,489
been seen for years,

314
00:12:17,489 --> 00:12:20,246
and the investigation found that the risk

315
00:12:20,246 --> 00:12:21,860
had been underestimated.

316
00:12:22,210 --> 00:12:25,868
Vajont changed dam engineering forever.

317
00:12:26,048 --> 00:12:28,911
Engineers now study the whole valley,

318
00:12:28,911 --> 00:12:30,227
not just the dam.

319
00:12:30,407 --> 00:12:32,436
The slopes around the lake,

320
00:12:32,436 --> 00:12:35,217
the geology, and how the rock behaves

321
00:12:35,217 --> 00:12:37,547
when the water rises and falls.

322
00:12:37,727 --> 00:12:40,206
The strongest dam in the world is

323
00:12:40,206 --> 00:12:42,759
useless if the mountain next to it

324
00:12:42,759 --> 00:12:43,811
is not stable.

325
00:12:44,160 --> 00:12:47,489
Why is the Itaipu Dam split exactly

326
00:12:47,489 --> 00:12:50,151
between Brazil and Paraguay?

327
00:12:50,331 --> 00:12:52,895
Because it sits on the Parana River,

328
00:12:52,895 --> 00:12:54,604
the border between them.

329
00:12:54,784 --> 00:12:57,547
The two countries built it together and

330
00:12:57,547 --> 00:12:58,894
own it fifty fifty.

331
00:12:59,074 --> 00:13:01,317
It has twenty generating units,

332
00:13:01,317 --> 00:13:04,429
half of them running on Brazil's electrical

333
00:13:04,429 --> 00:13:06,817
frequency and half on Paraguay's.

334
00:13:07,167 --> 00:13:10,809
Brazil uses sixty hertz electricity and Paraguay

335
00:13:10,809 --> 00:13:13,237
uses fifty, so the two halves of

336
00:13:13,237 --> 00:13:16,955
the plant produce power at different frequencies.

337
00:13:17,135 --> 00:13:19,785
Paraguay uses only a part of its

338
00:13:19,785 --> 00:13:22,436
share and sells most of the rest

339
00:13:22,436 --> 00:13:23,265
to Brazil.

340
00:13:23,445 --> 00:13:26,787
For years, Itaipu produced more electricity in

341
00:13:26,787 --> 00:13:29,258
a single year than any other plant

342
00:13:29,258 --> 00:13:29,911
on Earth.

343
00:13:30,261 --> 00:13:32,207
It shows that a dam is not

344
00:13:32,207 --> 00:13:34,302
only an engineering problem.

345
00:13:34,482 --> 00:13:36,433
It is a political one,

346
00:13:36,433 --> 00:13:37,498
a legal one,

347
00:13:37,498 --> 00:13:38,917
and a human one.

348
00:13:39,097 --> 00:13:41,774
Every curve, pipe and tunnel you have

349
00:13:41,774 --> 00:13:44,740
seen today exists because a whole country

350
00:13:44,740 --> 00:13:47,779
is downstream, trusting that the wall will

351
00:13:47,779 --> 00:13:48,141
hold.

352
00:13:48,490 --> 00:13:50,325
So here are the eleven.

353
00:13:50,505 --> 00:13:53,644
A curve that squeezes instead of bends,

354
00:13:53,644 --> 00:13:55,979
and a thick wall that wins by

355
00:13:55,979 --> 00:13:56,542
weight.

356
00:13:56,722 --> 00:13:59,218
Pipes that cooled the concrete,

357
00:13:59,218 --> 00:14:01,231
a giant slide for floods,

358
00:14:01,231 --> 00:14:03,888
and air slots that stop the water

359
00:14:03,888 --> 00:14:05,418
eating the tunnels.

360
00:14:05,598 --> 00:14:07,204
Towers in the lake,

361
00:14:07,204 --> 00:14:10,077
pipes big enough to drive through,

362
00:14:10,077 --> 00:14:12,021
tunnels inside the dam,

363
00:14:12,021 --> 00:14:13,373
stairs for fish,

364
00:14:13,373 --> 00:14:16,331
a mountain that fell into the lake,

365
00:14:16,331 --> 00:14:19,205
and a dam shared by two countries.

366
00:14:19,554 --> 00:14:21,210
Next time you see a dam,

367
00:14:21,210 --> 00:14:22,451
look at its shape,

368
00:14:22,451 --> 00:14:24,659
and you will know which trick it

369
00:14:24,659 --> 00:14:26,590
uses to hold the water back.

370
00:14:26,770 --> 00:14:29,683
If you want more machines and structures

371
00:14:29,683 --> 00:14:31,357
explained part by part,

372
00:14:31,357 --> 00:14:33,469
subscribe to Machine Reverse.

373
00:14:33,649 --> 00:14:35,489
And tell us in the comments what

374
